      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The `SettingWithCopyWarning` is a classic sign that pandas is unsure whether your filtered dataframe is a \"view\" (a temporary slice) or an \"independent\" copy, and it's trying to protect you from accidentally modifying data you didn't intend to touch. It's like a very cautious librarian who keeps asking, \"Are you sure you want to change this book?\" before letting you rewrite a page, because sometimes the book is just a photocopy of the original collection, and if you change the photocopy, the original collection gets messed up too.\n\nTo fix this headache in your production pipeline, you need to explicitly tell pandas, \"I want a fresh, independent copy of this data.\" The most reliable way is to use the `.copy()` method immediately after your filter. By doing `df_filtered = df[condition].copy()`, you create a brand new dataframe object that is completely separate from the source, ensuring that any subsequent operations on `df_filtered` won't trigger the warning or cause silent data corruption.\n\nThink of it like a team of construction workers (your code) trying to build a new wall on a plot of land. If they just point to a section of an existing wall and say, \"",
